drm/h713-afbd: magnify a smaller source onto the panel

Stage 2 of the only no-GPU route to 1080p on this 1280x720 panel.  The VE
downscales at decode time to 960x544 (it can only halve or quarter each axis),
and this patch takes that raster back up to the panel with the proc upscaler at
0x05180000.  Hardware-confirmed on the panel 2026-09-12; see
docs/handoff-2026-09-12-ve-scaledown.md.

Before this, the plane declared DRM_PLANE_NO_SCALING and rejected any
framebuffer that was not exactly 1280x720, so the decoder had nowhere to put a
smaller frame.

WHAT MADE THIS WORK IS PROC_IN_WIN AT 0x34, THE INPUT WINDOW.

Every earlier attempt at this block set a ratio and left the geometry alone.
0x34 reads 0x050002D0 -- {1280,720} -- on the live raster, so the block was
being told its input was panel-sized while the ratio said magnify, and it
confined the output to a hard-edged rectangle with flat grey around it.  That
looked like a broken scaler for two sessions.  Setting it to the real input
size makes the magnified picture fill the panel cleanly.

The other two things this block will punish:

  - Its registers carry live upper bits that belong to something else.
    PROC_RATIO_H reads 0x43010000 and PROC_PHASE_H reads 0x0F008000 on a
    running panel, so every field is read-modify-written.  A bare store
    clobbers state we have never decoded.

  - The V phase at unity is 0, not 0x10000: (unity + unity) >> 1 overflows the
    16-bit field.  The masks make that fall out correctly rather than by luck.

The block only ever magnifies -- ProcWinNode::CalcScaleRatio in the MIPS
firmware cannot express an above-unity ratio, the field clamps, and a measured
sweep of 0.8x/0.667x/0.5x was pixel-for-pixel identical to bypass -- so the
plane advertises 1:1 as its minimum scale and refuses anything that would need
shrinking.

Source geometry is now derived from the framebuffer rather than assumed: the
size, the 16x16 block count, both strides and the VideoInfo descriptor all
follow fb->width/height.  An inherited 1920x1088 in those registers is exactly
what once had the fetcher scanning past the end of a smaller buffer.

The registers the driver touches are saved at probe and written back verbatim
when video stops, so the 1280x720 RGB console that owns the panel afterwards
gets the block in the state the MIPS firmware left it.

Not yet exercised: a real 960x544 framebuffer has never been scanned out.  The
upscaler was validated by magnifying a 960x544 window of a 1280x720 buffer,
because the test tool hardcodes the panel size.  The configuration is
identical, but that gap closes with this patch, not before it.

